WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.404
One equation, two unknowns

2
00:00:05.404 --> 00:00:07.351
x plus y equals two.

3
00:00:07.351 --> 00:00:11.060
Every point on this line is a solution.

4
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:12.636
Two, zero works.

5
00:00:12.636 --> 00:00:16.160
So does zero, two, and so does three

6
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.180
minus one.

7
00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:18.338
Last time

8
00:00:18.338 --> 00:00:21.976
the right inverse picked the point one, one.

9
00:00:21.976 --> 00:00:24.564
Out of infinitely many answers

10
00:00:24.564 --> 00:00:27.636
is there a reason to prefer that one?

11
00:00:27.636 --> 00:00:29.010
Pause and think.

12
00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:35.642
Measure each answer by its length.

13
00:00:35.642 --> 00:00:37.976
Two, zero has length two.

14
00:00:37.976 --> 00:00:40.669
One, one has length root two

15
00:00:40.669 --> 00:00:43.003
about one point four one.

16
00:00:43.093 --> 00:00:46.414
Grow a circle around the origin until

17
00:00:46.504 --> 00:00:48.838
it first touches the line

18
00:00:48.838 --> 00:00:51.710
it touches exactly at one, one.

19
00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:52.884
Why there?

20
00:00:52.884 --> 00:00:56.262
One, one points along the row, one, one

21
00:00:56.262 --> 00:00:58.239
it is in the row space.

22
00:00:58.321 --> 00:01:00.381
Every other solution adds

23
00:01:00.463 --> 00:01:02.276
a step along the line

24
00:01:02.276 --> 00:01:04.253
a null space direction

25
00:01:04.253 --> 00:01:06.230
at a right angle to it.

26
00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:07.466
By Pythagoras

27
00:01:07.466 --> 00:01:10.020
that step can only add length.

28
00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:13.727
That point is x plus, A plus times b

29
00:01:13.727 --> 00:01:17.448
the solution that lies in the row space

30
00:01:17.448 --> 00:01:20.170
and the shortest of them all.

31
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.922
Now both problems at once.

32
00:01:22.922 --> 00:01:26.013
The rank one matrix B from part one

33
00:01:26.013 --> 00:01:29.021
and the right side one, two, three.

34
00:01:29.021 --> 00:01:31.611
It is not in the column space

35
00:01:31.611 --> 00:01:33.950
there is no exact solution.

36
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:35.289
First best

37
00:01:35.289 --> 00:01:38.436
get B x as close to c as possible.

38
00:01:38.526 --> 00:01:40.864
The smallest miss is about

39
00:01:40.954 --> 00:01:42.932
zero point seven five.

40
00:01:43.022 --> 00:01:46.080
But a whole line of inputs reaches

41
00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:47.878
that closest point.

42
00:01:47.878 --> 00:01:51.835
Second best: among them, take the shortest.

43
00:01:51.835 --> 00:01:55.162
That is x plus, zero point two four

44
00:01:55.162 --> 00:01:57.140
zero point four nine.

45
00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:00.327
The circle around the origin touches

46
00:02:00.406 --> 00:02:02.061
the line right there

47
00:02:02.061 --> 00:02:05.213
on the row space line through one, two.

48
00:02:05.213 --> 00:02:08.050
Least squares first, then shortest.

49
00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:11.693
Is any point on that line just as good?

50
00:02:11.693 --> 00:02:14.128
Add the null space vector two

51
00:02:14.128 --> 00:02:15.778
minus one to x plus.

52
00:02:15.778 --> 00:02:18.841
B sends it to exactly the same output

53
00:02:18.841 --> 00:02:21.590
so it misses c by the same amount.

54
00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:24.712
But its length is two point three

55
00:02:24.712 --> 00:02:27.891
about four times the length of x plus.

56
00:02:27.891 --> 00:02:29.929
Same fit, longer answer.

57
00:02:29.929 --> 00:02:32.700
The pseudoinverse never picks it.

58
00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.104
The rule says: invert the singular values

59
00:02:37.197 --> 00:02:38.876
that are not zero.

60
00:02:38.876 --> 00:02:40.369
Here is a trap.

61
00:02:40.462 --> 00:02:43.540
This matrix N squashes the circle

62
00:02:43.633 --> 00:02:46.524
onto an almost perfect segment.

63
00:02:46.524 --> 00:02:49.229
Its singular values are two

64
00:02:49.229 --> 00:02:52.960
and five times ten to the minus eleven.

65
00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.641
It is tempting to invert both

66
00:02:55.641 --> 00:02:58.051
since neither is exactly zero.

67
00:02:58.128 --> 00:03:00.537
Then a change of one thousandth

68
00:03:00.615 --> 00:03:03.335
in the right side throws the answer

69
00:03:03.413 --> 00:03:05.356
out to about ten million

70
00:03:05.356 --> 00:03:06.910
far off the screen.

71
00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.987
That tiny sigma is rounding noise for zero.

72
00:03:10.987 --> 00:03:14.453
Cut it off, and the answer is one point

73
00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:16.187
zero zero zero two

74
00:03:16.187 --> 00:03:19.827
one point zero zero zero three: sensible

75
00:03:19.827 --> 00:03:20.780
and short.

76
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.051
This matters in machine learning.

77
00:03:24.051 --> 00:03:26.497
Three samples, five features

78
00:03:26.497 --> 00:03:29.677
three equations, five unknown weights.

79
00:03:29.759 --> 00:03:32.205
Infinitely many models fit the

80
00:03:32.286 --> 00:03:34.080
training data exactly.

81
00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:36.821
We cannot draw five dimensions

82
00:03:36.821 --> 00:03:39.906
but the picture is the one from before

83
00:03:39.906 --> 00:03:41.912
a flat set of exact fits.

84
00:03:41.989 --> 00:03:44.071
The pseudoinverse picks its

85
00:03:44.148 --> 00:03:46.925
point of smallest length, about two.

86
00:03:47.002 --> 00:03:48.776
It still reproduces all

87
00:03:48.853 --> 00:03:50.550
three targets exactly.

88
00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:54.163
Ridge regression adds a penalty

89
00:03:54.163 --> 00:03:57.765
lambda times the squared length of w.

90
00:03:57.765 --> 00:04:01.746
In SVD terms, it replaces one over sigma

91
00:04:01.746 --> 00:04:04.684
which explodes for tiny sigma

92
00:04:04.684 --> 00:04:08.570
by sigma over sigma squared plus lambda.

93
00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.821
For large sigma the two curves agree.

94
00:04:11.899 --> 00:04:14.330
For small sigma the ridge curve

95
00:04:14.408 --> 00:04:16.290
drops back towards zero

96
00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:18.171
instead of shooting up.

97
00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:20.523
It is a smooth way of cutting

98
00:04:20.602 --> 00:04:22.640
off small singular values.

99
00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:25.221
And as lambda shrinks to zero

100
00:04:25.295 --> 00:04:27.452
the ridge weights move to the

101
00:04:27.526 --> 00:04:29.162
pseudoinverse weights

102
00:04:29.162 --> 00:04:31.764
the distance falls from about one

103
00:04:31.764 --> 00:04:34.590
to two hundredths, to two millionths.

104
00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:37.623
A check for working with AI.

105
00:04:37.711 --> 00:04:41.041
Suppose an assistant solves B x equals

106
00:04:41.128 --> 00:04:43.581
c with the normal equations

107
00:04:43.581 --> 00:04:46.210
the inverse of B transpose B

108
00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:48.050
times B transpose c.

109
00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.786
B transpose B is singular

110
00:04:50.786 --> 00:04:52.529
so the code fails

111
00:04:52.529 --> 00:04:56.108
or with rounding returns huge numbers.

112
00:04:56.108 --> 00:04:58.311
A least squares solver

113
00:04:58.311 --> 00:05:01.614
or the pseudoinverse, gives x plus

114
00:05:01.614 --> 00:05:05.560
zero point two four, zero point four nine.

115
00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:08.281
So A plus times b is the

116
00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.422
best answer twice over

117
00:05:10.422 --> 00:05:13.991
the least squares fit, and among those

118
00:05:13.991 --> 00:05:15.240
the shortest.

119
00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:18.273
Cut off the tiny singular values

120
00:05:18.273 --> 00:05:20.860
or let ridge do it smoothly.

121
00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:24.307
Next lecture measures how dangerous

122
00:05:24.395 --> 00:05:25.899
a small sigma is

123
00:05:25.899 --> 00:05:28.730
norms and the condition number.
